    "title": "pandas.tseries.holiday.AbstractHolidayCalendar.holidays — pandas 3.1.0.dev0 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.tseries.holiday.AbstractHolidayCalendar.holidays#\n\n- \nAbstractHolidayCalendar.holidays(*start=None* ,*end=None* ,*return_name=False* )[source]#\n- Return a curve with holidays between start_date and end_date. The holidays for the calendar are computed by evaluating the `dates` of each rule in`self.rules` and merging the results,\ncaching them so that repeated calls covering a previously\nrequested date range do not need to be recomputed.\n  - Parameters:\n    - **start** starting date, datetime-like, optional\n    - The earliest date to consider, defaults to `AbstractHolidayCalendar.start_date` .\n    - **end** ending date, datetime-like, optional\n    - The latest date to consider, defaults to `AbstractHolidayCalendar.end_date` .\n    - **return_name** bool, optional\n    - If True, return a series that has dates and holiday names. False will only return a DatetimeIndex of dates.\n  - Returns:\n    - DatetimeIndex or Series\n    - DatetimeIndex of holidays, or a Series mapping holiday dates to their names if `return_name` is True.\n See also \n  - `tseries.holiday.Holiday.dates`\n  - Calculate holidays observed between start date and end date.\n Examples >>> from pandas.tseries.holiday import AbstractHolidayCalendar, USLaborDay >>> class MyCalendar(AbstractHolidayCalendar): ... rules = [USLaborDay] >>> MyCalendar().holidays(start=\"2020-01-01\", end=\"2020-12-31\") DatetimeIndex(['2020-09-07'], dtype='datetime64[us]', freq=None)",
    "markdown_length": 1441,
